### LogiFlow API — the whole flow, top to bottom.
###
### Works in VS Code (REST Client extension), Visual Studio 2022+, and Rider.
### Click "Send Request" above each block, in order.
###
### Start the stack first:
###   docker compose up -d
###   cd src/LogiFlow.Api && dotnet run
###
### No Docker? If SQL Server or LocalDB is installed locally:
###   cd src/LogiFlow.Api && dotnet run --launch-profile no-docker

@host = http://localhost:5199
@contentType = application/json

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 1. Health — no auth required
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Liveness: is the process up? Deliberately checks NO dependencies.
GET {{host}}/health/live

### Readiness: can it serve traffic? Checks SQL Server.
GET {{host}}/health/ready

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 2. Authentication
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### A protected endpoint with no token. Expect 401.
GET {{host}}/api/orders

### Mint a development token. NOT a login system — no password check, no user store.
### A real deployment delegates this to Entra ID / Auth0 / Keycloak and deletes this endpoint.
# @name token
POST {{host}}/api/dev/token
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "email": "kristi@logiflow.dev",
  "roles": ["Admin"]
}

###
@auth = Bearer {{token.response.body.accessToken}}

### Now it works. Expect 200 with an empty first page.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
Authorization: {{auth}}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 3. Catalogue — public reads, protected writes
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Anonymous: the catalogue is public. Cached for 5 minutes at the HTTP layer.
# @name product
GET {{host}}/api/products/ELE-100001

###
@productId = {{product.response.body.id}}

### Create a product. Requires the CatalogManager policy.
POST {{host}}/api/products
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sku": "ELE-777001",
  "name": "Handheld RFID Scanner",
  "description": "Bluetooth, 12h battery",
  "unitPrice": 249.00,
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "weightGrams": 410
}

### Send that again — the unique index rejects it. Expect 409 with code Product.DuplicateSku.
POST {{host}}/api/products
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sku": "ELE-777001",
  "name": "Handheld RFID Scanner",
  "description": null,
  "unitPrice": 249.00,
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "weightGrams": 410
}

### Invalid SKU format. Expect 400 with a per-field `errors` object.
POST {{host}}/api/products
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sku": "nope",
  "name": "Bad SKU",
  "description": null,
  "unitPrice": 10.00,
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "weightGrams": 100
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 4. The order lifecycle
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### You need a customer id. Grab one from the seeded data:
###   docker exec logiflow-sql /opt/mssql-tools18/bin/sqlcmd -S localhost -U sa \
###     -P 'Str0ng!Passw0rd' -C -d LogiFlow -Q "SELECT TOP 3 Id, CompanyName, Tier FROM logiflow.Customers"
###
### Paste it below. Tier 1 = Standard (0% off), Tier 4 = Platinum (15% off) —
### try both and compare the totals.

@customerId = PASTE-A-CUSTOMER-ID-HERE

### Open a draft order. Expect 201 with a Location header.
# @name order
POST {{host}}/api/orders
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "customerId": "{{customerId}}",
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "shippingAddress": {
    "line1": "Via Roma 10",
    "line2": null,
    "city": "Milano",
    "region": null,
    "postalCode": "20100",
    "countryCode": "IT"
  }
}

###
@orderId = {{order.response.body.$}}

### Add 3 units.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/lines
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 3
}

### Add 2 MORE of the same product. This MERGES into one line of 5 — it does not
### create a second line. That is a business decision, documented in Order.AddLine.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/lines
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 2
}

### Read it back. Check: lines.length == 1, quantity == 5.
### Also note `allowedTransitions` — the API tells the client what it may do next, so the
### UI never has to reimplement the state machine in TypeScript and get it subtly wrong.
GET {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Validation failure: quantity 0. Expect 400 with errors.Quantity.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/lines
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 0
}

### Submit. This is the interesting one: Order.Submit() raises OrderSubmittedDomainEvent,
### which reserves warehouse stock IN THE SAME TRANSACTION, which itself raises
### StockReservedDomainEvent, and writes outbox rows for all of them.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/submit
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Now try to edit it. Expect 409 — the state machine refuses. Note `code`:
### "Order.NotEditable", which is what a client should branch on.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/lines
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 1
}

### Cancel it. Releases the reserved stock back to the warehouse that holds it —
### which the order recorded in FulfillingWarehouseId when it reserved.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/cancel
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "reason": "Customer changed their mind"
}

### Cancel it again. Expect 409 — Cancelled is a terminal state.
POST {{host}}/api/orders/{{orderId}}/cancel
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "reason": "Trying twice"
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 5. Search — composable filters, stable pagination
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### No parameters: every filter is optional and the defaults are applied server-side.
GET {{host}}/api/orders
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Filtered and sorted. Only the predicates you supply appear in the SQL.
GET {{host}}/api/orders?status=Submitted&sortBy=Total&sortDirection=Descending&pageSize=5
Authorization: {{auth}}

### The DoS guard: ask for a million rows, get 100. PageSize is clamped server-side.
GET {{host}}/api/orders?pageSize=1000000
Authorization: {{auth}}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 6. Inventory
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### Get a warehouse id:
###   ... -Q "SELECT Id, Code FROM logiflow.Warehouses"

@warehouseId = PASTE-A-WAREHOUSE-ID-HERE

### Live stock levels. DELIBERATELY never cached — a stale stock figure produces
### orders that cannot be fulfilled.
GET {{host}}/api/inventory/warehouses/{{warehouseId}}/products/{{productId}}
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Book in a supplier delivery.
POST {{host}}/api/inventory/receipts
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "warehouseId": "{{warehouseId}}",
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "quantity": 250
}

### Apply a stock take. A reason is REQUIRED — stock adjustments are financially
### material and a common vector for internal theft, so they are always audited.
POST {{host}}/api/inventory/adjustments
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "warehouseId": "{{warehouseId}}",
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "actualQuantity": 240,
  "reason": "Quarterly stock take — 10 units damaged in transit"
}

### Adjust below what is already reserved. Expect 409 — the model refuses to promise
### units that do not exist.
POST {{host}}/api/inventory/adjustments
Authorization: {{auth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "warehouseId": "{{warehouseId}}",
  "productId": "{{productId}}",
  "actualQuantity": 0,
  "reason": "Testing the guard"
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 7. Reporting — the advanced LINQ from module 09
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
###
### NOTE: dates must be URL-encoded. A raw '+' in a query string means a SPACE,
### so a '+02:00' offset arrives mangled. Use Z (UTC) or encode '+' as %2B.
### Getting this wrong returns 400, not 500 — see the BadHttpRequestException arm
### in GlobalExceptionHandler.

### Revenue bucketed by month. GROUP BY + SUM + COUNT, all in SQL.
### Cancelled and draft orders are excluded from revenue.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/sales?from=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z&to=2027-01-01T00:00:00Z&period=Monthly
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Also try period=Daily, Weekly, Quarterly.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/sales?from=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z&to=2027-01-01T00:00:00Z&period=Quarterly
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Best sellers. JOIN + GROUP BY + COUNT(DISTINCT OrderId) + ranking.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/top-products?from=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z&to=2027-01-01T00:00:00Z&take=5
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Customer lifetime value. Count/Sum/Average/Max/Min over ONE grouping,
### then HAVING COUNT(*) >= @minimumOrders.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/customers?minimumOrders=1
Authorization: {{auth}}

### Products at or below their reorder threshold, worst shortfall first.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/low-stock
Authorization: {{auth}}

### A malformed date. Expect 400 (NOT 500) with a helpful message.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/sales?from=not-a-date&to=2027-01-01T00:00:00Z&period=Monthly
Authorization: {{auth}}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 8. Authorization policies
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Get a token with ONLY the Analyst role.
# @name analystToken
POST {{host}}/api/dev/token
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "email": "analyst@logiflow.dev",
  "roles": ["Analyst"]
}

###
@analystAuth = Bearer {{analystToken.response.body.accessToken}}

### An Analyst may read reports. Expect 200.
GET {{host}}/api/reports/low-stock
Authorization: {{analystAuth}}

### An Analyst may NOT write to the catalogue. Expect 403 — NOT 401.
### 401 means "I do not know who you are"; 403 means "I know exactly who you are
### and you still cannot". Returning 401 here would send clients into a login loop.
POST {{host}}/api/products
Authorization: {{analystAuth}}
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "sku": "ELE-888001",
  "name": "Should be forbidden",
  "description": null,
  "unitPrice": 10.00,
  "currencyCode": "EUR",
  "weightGrams": 100
}

### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
### 9. The carrier webhook
### ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

### Idempotent by design: carriers retry aggressively, sometimes for days.
### A duplicate must return success, not 409 — otherwise the carrier retries forever
### and someone gets paged at 3am for a parcel that arrived fine.
### Anonymous, because a carrier has never seen your identity provider.
POST {{host}}/api/shipments/webhooks/delivery
Content-Type: {{contentType}}

{
  "trackingNumber": "1Z999AA10123456784",
  "signedBy": "M. Rossi"
}
